:: day_5_15_kira
<<nobr>>
<<set $day_step to 15>>
<<set $locationName to "Kira's Apartment">>
<<set $locationImage to "images/locations/kira_apartment.jpeg">>
<</nobr>>

The elevator. Third floor. 3C. The door is unlocked, like she said. I push it open.

The apartment smells like home. Not my home   a real home. Soy sauce and mirin and simmering onion and something sweet from the rice cooker. Kira is at the stove, her back to me, stirring a pot. She's wearing an oversized cream sweater and black leggings and her hair is clipped up with a claw clip, the bob lifted off her neck, the back of her neck bare and pale and   I file the image. Kira Facts, Volume 83: the back of her neck when the hair is up.

<img src="images/scenes/day_5/day_5_1.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment" class="scene-image">

Packet is on the counter beside the stove, sitting upright, watching the pot with the unwavering focus of a cat who believes his involvement is required for the meal to succeed.

<<kira "Hey! Come in, close the door, it's   are those flowers?">>

She turns from the stove. Spatula in hand. Steam rising behind her. Her eyes land on the brown paper bundle in my hand and something happens to her face   a softening, a widening, the specific expression of a person receiving something they didn't expect from someone they didn't expect it from.

<<eli "Sweet peas. They mean gratitude.">>

<<kira "You bought me flowers.">>

<<eli "The lady at the shop said they mean 'thank you for a lovely time.'">>

<<kira "Eli.">>

<<eli "What?">>

<<kira "You bought me flowers.">>

She says it twice because once wasn't enough to process it. The spatula drips onto the floor. Packet eyes the drip with interest. She doesn't notice. She's looking at the flowers and then at me and then at the flowers and the expression on her face is   I don't have the word for it. Joy isn't big enough. Surprise isn't accurate enough. It's the face of a person who has been sending red hearts into an inbox for a year and a half and just received one back in botanical form.

She crosses the kitchen in four steps and wraps her arms around me   flowers crushed between us, the brown paper crinkling, the sweet peas pressed against my chest and her chest simultaneously. She's small. Her head is against my collarbone. I can feel her breathing speed up and then deliberately slow, the self-regulation of a person who is having an emotion and choosing to feel it rather than perform it.

<<kira "Thank you.">>

<<eli "Thank you. For   everything.">>

<<kira "Don't make me cry before the gyudon is done. It'll get salty.">>

She pulls back. Takes the flowers. Finds a glass from the kitchen cabinet   she doesn't have a vase, because who has a vase at twenty-one   and fills it with water and puts the sweet peas in and sets them on the counter beside the stove. They look correct there. Small and delicate in a glass next to a pot of simmering beef and onion.

She turns back to me. Pushes the glasses up. Looks at my face.

Three seconds.

<<kira "Eli.">>

<<eli "Yeah?">>

<<kira "Your nose.">>

Not "did you do something?" Not "you look different." Direct. Specific. The analytical engine identifying the modification with the precision of a person who has been studying faces on screens for two years and knows when bone structure changes.

My heart hammers. The moment is here. The moment I've been rehearsing since the procedure chair, since the latte, since the walk through the January evening with gratitude-flowers in my hand.

<<eli "Yeah. My nose.">>

<<kira "It's different.">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

<<kira "That's not a skincare routine.">>

<<eli "No. It's not.">>

She looks at me. The glasses catching the kitchen light. The steam from the pot curling behind her. Packet on the counter, observing. The sweet peas in their glass. The gyudon bubbling softly on the stove. The whole apartment holding its breath.

<<eli "Can we sit down? I need to tell you something.">>

[[Sit down|day_5_16_kira]]

:: day_5_16_kira
<<set $day_step to 16>>

She turns the stove to low. Wipes her hands on a dish towel. Follows me to the couch. We sit   not touching, a foot of space between us, the geography of a conversation that needs room.

Packet leaps from the counter and settles on the armrest. Observing. The feline witness to whatever happens next.

<<kira "Okay. Talk.">>

She says it the way she says things when she's decided to listen   jaw set, eyes steady, the streamer mask gone, just Kira, just the person underneath. I've seen this expression twice before: once when she told me the truth about the streaming economy, and once when she said "I've liked you since the first time we played together." The expression that means the guard is down and the brain is open and whatever I say next will be received by the full, unfiltered weight of her attention.

I take a breath. The rehearsed versions evaporate. The crafted openings dissolve. What's left is the raw thing, the unshaped truth, and I open my mouth and let it out the way you let out a breath you've been holding too long.

<<eli "You told me the system rewards a specific input. Female. Cute. On camera. You told me in this apartment, sitting in that chair, with the ring light off and the afternoon light on your glasses. You said if you were a guy with the same personality and the same humor you'd be me. You said the biggest reason is your face.">>

<<kira "I remember.">>

<<eli "I hacked it.">>

Silence. Her eyes on mine. Not blinking. Not pushing her glasses up. Not fidgeting.

<<eli "I made a second account. ElliePlayz. I installed a voice modulator   software that makes my voice sound female. I went live as a girl. Voice only at first. No face, no cam, just the voice and a pink background and a name that's two letters from mine.">>

<<kira "When?">>

<<eli "Three days ago. The day after you told me the truth.">>

<<kira "Three days.">>

<<eli "I made forty-seven dollars in the first hour. The same number as my bank balance. Like the universe was rhyming.">>

She's still. The stillness of a person who is processing, who is running the information through every filter she has   the pragmatist, the streamer, the girlfriend, the person who said "trust is the most valuable currency" over pad thai.

<<eli "Then I went to a clinic. RegenaForm. Four blocks from my apartment. They do   they modify your face. With your own DNA. No surgery, no scars. The eyelashes you noticed   those are from them. The brows and today   this afternoon, three hours ago   the nose.">>

I gesture at my face. At the evidence. The lashes she said "hmm" about. The brows she squinted at. The nose she identified in three seconds.

<<eli "I've been streaming with a wig and the voice modulator and the face modifications and I've earned five hundred and four dollars in four days. Eight hundred and seventy-six after this morning. More than EliPlays earned in eight months.">>

The numbers hang in the air between us. $876. The gap between what I am and what the system rewards, measured in dollars and closed by deception.

<<kira "The 'streaming stuff.'">>

<<eli "The streaming stuff.">>

<<kira "The 'new approaches.'">>

<<eli "The new approaches.">>

She nods. Slow. Once. The nod of a person who is clicking puzzle pieces into a frame she suspected but didn't fully see.

<<kira "The skincare routine.">>

<<eli "Not a skincare routine.">>

<<kira "I knew it wasn't a skincare routine. The first time. In the car. I knew.">>

<<eli "I know you knew.">>

<<kira "And the brows. The 'drugstore grooming kit.'">>

<<eli "RegenaForm. Three hundred dollars. I just paid it off today.">>

She takes her glasses off. Holds them in both hands, the way she does when she's closest to being undefended. The brown eyes, unframed. Looking at me the way she looked at me before the first kiss   focused, terrified, committed. Except this time the terrified part is mine.

<<kira "Eli.">>

<<eli "Yeah?">>

<<kira "Are you okay?">>

Not "how could you." Not "you lied to me." Not "you're pretending to be a woman for money." The first thing she asks is if I'm okay. The question of a person whose instinct, even in the face of a confession that implicates her trust, is to check the person confessing.

<<eli "I don't know. I think so. The money is real. The numbers work. But the   the lying. To you. Every time you noticed something and I made up an excuse. Every red heart I sent from a phone that had a wig in the closet and a voice modulator on the desktop. That part is not okay.">>

<<kira "No. That part is not okay.">>

She says it without anger. With the flat, clean clarity of a person naming a fact. The sky is blue. Water is wet. Lying to your girlfriend about restructuring your face is not okay.

<<eli "I was going to tell you after the nose. That was the plan. 'After the nose.' But the plan before that was 'after the brows' and before that was 'after the first stream.' It was always 'after the next thing.' The postponement was the pattern.">>

<<kira "But you're telling me now.">>

<<eli "I'm telling you now.">>

<<kira "Before the next thing.">>

<<eli "Before the next thing.">>

She puts the glasses back on. The gesture is deliberate   the reframing, the refocusing, the return to clarity. She looks at me with the glasses on and the expression behind them is not what I expected.

She's not angry. She's not disgusted. She's not crying. She's   thinking. The analytical engine is running, processing the data, sorting the inputs. The pragmatist is doing what the pragmatist does: assessing the situation before reacting to it.

<<kira "Show me.">>

<<eli "Show you what?">>

<<kira "The dashboard. ElliePlayz. Show me the numbers.">>

[[Show the dashboard|day_5_17_kira]]

:: day_5_17_kira
<<set $day_step to 17>>

I pull out my phone. Open the browser. Navigate to the ElliePlayz dashboard.

Total Followers: 300
Total Earnings: $876
Peak Viewers: 339
Subscribers: 14

She takes the phone. Scrolls through the stats the way she scrolls through her own dashboard   analytically, methodically, the numbers telling a story she reads fluently. I watch her eyes move across the screen   the same eyes that read $487 on her own dashboard five days ago and told me the truth about the system.

<<kira "Three hundred followers in four days.">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

<<kira "Eight hundred seventy-six dollars.">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

<<kira "Three hundred and thirty-nine peak.">>

She hands the phone back. Sits back on the couch. Pulls her knees up, wraps her arms around them   the self-contained posture of a person who is processing something large and needs to physically hold herself together while she does it.

<<kira "You proved my theory.">>

<<eli "Your theory proved itself. I just provided the data.">>

<<kira "Same voice. Same skill. Same person. Different input.">>

<<eli "Different input.">>

She's quiet for thirty seconds. The gyudon bubbles on low. Packet purrs on the armrest. The sweet peas stand in their water glass, pale and delicate, the gratitude-flowers watching.

<<kira "I'm not going to blame you.">>

<<eli "You could.">>

<<kira "I could. But I'm the one who told you the system was broken. I'm the one who showed you my dashboard and said 'the biggest reason is this' and pointed at my face. I gave you the diagnosis. I can't be surprised you found the prescription.">>

<<eli "That doesn't make the lying okay.">>

<<kira "No. It doesn't. The lying was shitty. Don't do it again.">>

She says "don't do it again" the way she sets boundaries on stream   once, firmly, and then the topic is closed. A rule established. A line drawn. Not negotiable.

<<eli "I won't.">>

<<kira "I mean it, Eli. No more 'skincare routine.' No more 'grooming kit.' If you're modifying your face, I want to know. If you're streaming as Ellie, I want to know. I don't have to understand it. I don't have to like it. But I need to know.">>

<<eli "Okay.">>

<<kira "Okay.">>

She unfolds from the knee-wrapped posture. Her legs come down. She shifts on the couch, the space between us narrowing from a foot to six inches. Her hand finds my knee. Rests there. The pixel-heart tattoo visible at her wrist.

<<kira "I'm not going to tell you to stop.">>

<<eli "You're not?">>

<<kira "It's your face. It's your career. If this is what you want   if this is the thing that gets you out of the ceiling crack and the microwave burritos and the four viewers and DarkSlayer   then this is what you want. I'm okay with you, Eli. Or Ellie. Or whatever combination you end up being.">>

<<eli "You mean that?">>

<<kira "I'm bisexual. I literally told you I like softness. If your face gets softer, I'm not going to suddenly find you unattractive. That's not how this works for me.">>

She pushes her glasses up. The nervous gesture. But when her hand comes down, it finds my face instead of her lap. Her fingertips on my cheek. Her thumb tracing the line of the new nose   the bridge, the slope, the refined tip. She traces it slowly, the way she traces condensation on beer glasses and circles on the back of my hand. Learning the new geography.

<<kira "It's a nice nose.">>

<<eli "Yeah?">>

<<kira "It suits your eyes. The proportions work.">>

<<eli "That's what Dr. Maren said. 'The proportions are harmonizing.'">>

<<kira "Dr. Maren sounds like an architect.">>

<<eli "They kind of are.">>

Her thumb moves from the nose to the brow. Traces the arch. The feathered shape that opens my eye area. Then down to the lashes   her fingertip touching them gently, feeling their length, the dense dark fringe that Dr. Maren's bioactive compound grew from dormant cells.

<<kira "The lashes are incredible. I was so jealous when I first noticed them. I thought you just had secret amazing lashes and I'd never paid attention.">>

<<eli "They're mine. Technically. Grown from my own DNA.">>

<<kira "Science is wild.">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

She drops her hand. But the space between us has closed further. Her knee is touching mine. Her body is angled toward me on the couch, the same orientation as the izakaya, the same orientation as the car before the first kiss, the geometry of a person who has turned toward someone and chosen to keep facing them.

<<kira "But Eli   listen. I need to say something and I need you to actually hear it.">>

<<eli "I'm listening.">>

<<kira "You don't need to do this for money. You don't need to change your face to pay for brunch. I can make money for us. I make enough. You know I make enough.">>

<<eli "Kira ">>

<<kira "Let me finish. I'm not saying your streaming as Ellie is wrong. I'm saying the reason matters. If you're doing this because you want to   because something about it feels right, because the performance gives you something, because Ellie is a part of you that wants to exist   that's one thing. But if you're doing it purely because you can't afford to eat and you think this is the only way? That's a different thing and I need you to be honest about which one it is.">>

I sit with the question. It lands in the center of my chest and sits there, heavy and warm, demanding an answer I haven't given myself yet. Why am I doing this? The money is the obvious answer. The $876. The brunch. The paid-off debt. The nose. The loop. But beneath the money there's something else   something that surfaced when I first heard Ellie's voice come through the speakers and it sounded not just female but viable. Not just different but better. Not better as in superior to Eli. Better as in more effective at existing in the world.

<<eli "Both. It's both. I need the money. I'm not going to pretend I don't need the money   I have a ceiling crack and a mattress on the floor and I've been eating burritos for eight months. The money matters. But it's also... I don't know how to say this.">>

<<kira "Try.">>

<<eli "When I'm Ellie, people listen. Not because I'm a girl   because I'm a girl who's funny and shy and trying. The same trying I do as Eli. But as Eli, nobody sees it. As Ellie, the trying is visible. The trying gets rewarded and being rewarded for trying instead of punished for failing   that does something to a person. It changes the trying itself.">>

<<kira "It makes you try harder.">>

<<eli "It makes me try like I believe it might work.">>

She's quiet. Her hand on my knee. The gyudon on low. The apartment warm and steady around us.

<<kira "Okay.">>

<<eli "Okay?">>

<<kira "Okay. I hear you. I don't fully understand it. I might not fully understand it for a while. But I hear you and I'm not going anywhere.">>

<<eli "You're not?">>

<<kira "Eli. I kissed you in a parked car. I fell asleep on your shoulder for forty-five minutes. I walked out of a bathroom in a bathrobe because I wanted you to look at me. I'm not going anywhere because you changed your nose.">>

<<eli "What about the voice? The wig? The fake name?">>

<<kira "We'll figure it out. Together. But no more lies. That's the deal. No more lies and we figure it out together.">>

<<eli "Deal.">>

<<kira "Now come eat gyudon before Packet does.">>

[[Eat gyudon|day_5_18_kira]]

:: day_5_18_kira
<<set $day_step to 18>>

The gyudon is perfect.

Not in the restaurant sense   not the precise, Instagram-plated, seventeen-dollar bowl of curated ingredients. Perfect in the Kira sense. A generous mound of white rice in a ceramic bowl, topped with thinly sliced beef that's been simmered in soy sauce and mirin and dashi until it's soft and sweet and falling apart, ribbons of caramelized onion tangled through the meat, a soft-poached egg nestled on top with the yolk trembling when the bowl moves. She sprinkles togarashi over mine   "you said medium spice, I'm upgrading you to brave"   and benishoga on hers, the pink pickled ginger bright against the dark sauce.

We eat at her small kitchen table. Across from each other. The sweet peas between us in their water glass, pale pink and white against the brown paper that Kira carefully folded and tucked beneath the arrangement like a nest. Packet sits on the fourth chair   the chair that doesn't have a person   watching both bowls with the patience of an apex predator waiting for the herd to sleep.

The food is warm and deep. The beef melts. The egg yolk runs into the rice when I break it and the golden stream mixes with the soy-dark sauce and creates something that tastes like being cared for. I eat slowly. Not because the food deserves it   it does, but that's not why. I eat slowly because the confession has lifted something from my chest and the lifting has made room for other sensations   taste, warmth, the presence of a person across a table who knows the truth and is still here.

<<kira "Is it okay? I think I over-simmered the onions.">>

<<eli "The onions are perfect.">>

<<kira "They're mushy.">>

<<eli "Mushy is a texture choice.">>

<<kira "Mushy is what happens when you get distracted reading about parasocial economics while the stove is on.">>

<<eli "You were reading about parasocial economics while cooking?">>

<<kira "It was a really good article! and the onions suffered for it.">>

<<eli "The onions died doing what they loved.">>

She kicks me under the table. The bare-foot kick, the one from the ramen shop, the touch disguised as violence. Her toes connect with my ankle and stay, pressing warmly, the point of contact that means I'm here, I'm touching you, the food is mushy and the truth is out and I'm still here.

We eat. We talk. Not about Ellie. Not about RegenaForm or voice modulators or dashboards. About her mom's bakery and the mochi she sent. About Marcus and Devon and the AirPods that went down the drain. About Frieren   we're three episodes behind and she insists we catch up this weekend. About Packet, who has abandoned his chair and relocated to Kira's lap, where he curls into a circle and purrs with the smug satisfaction of a cat who has claimed the warmest surface in the room.

The conversation spirals the way it always does with Kira   touching and releasing, each topic orbiting the last. But underneath the spiral, there's a new current. A steadiness. The absence of the splinter   not gone entirely, not dissolved, but repositioned. The guilt has shifted from "I'm hiding this" to "I told the truth and the truth didn't destroy us." The splinter is still there. But it's been relocated from the center of my chest to somewhere further out, toward the edges, where it hurts less and matters less and might, eventually, dissolve entirely.

The bowls empty. She refuses to let me wash them   "you brought flowers, Eli, that's enough contribution for one evening"   and I sit on the couch while she rinses the dishes and I watch her from behind the way I watched her in the kitchen earlier. The claw clip. The bare neck. The sweater sleeves pushed to the elbows, her forearms visible, the pixel-heart tattoo vivid against the soap foam.

She dries her hands. Comes to the couch. Sits close. Her thigh against mine. Her shoulder against my arm. The position that's becoming a template, the default geometry of two people who have decided to occupy the same space.

<<kira "Hey.">>

<<eli "Hey.">>

<<kira "Can I look at it? The nose? Up close?">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

She takes her glasses off. Leans in. Her face comes close   four inches, three, the distance where the world becomes two faces and the space between them. Her eyes move over my nose with the careful attention of a person examining a painting in a gallery. The bridge. The slope. The tip. She reaches up and traces it again   her fingertip following the line Dr. Maren drew, the straight profile that replaced the crooked one.

<<kira "It's really well done. The bridge is so straight and the way it connects to your brow line   the whole upper face flows now. Before there was this break, like the nose was a different face from the eyes. Now it's one thing.">>

<<eli "You sound like Dr. Maren.">>

<<kira "Maybe I should've been a facial architect.">>

Her finger traces from the nose tip to my upper lip. Stops there. The boundary. The line where the modifications end and Eli begins. She looks at my lips   the thin, pale lips that are still the originals, the feature that lives below the crop line, below the wig frame, below the zone that's been feminized.

<<kira "These are still you.">>

<<eli "These are still me.">>

<<kira "Good.">>

She kisses me.

Not the hello kiss. Not the goodnight kiss. Not the post-Frieren couch kiss or the izakaya car kiss. A new kiss. A kiss that exists on the other side of the truth, in the territory where she knows everything and has chosen to stay. Her lips on mine   on the lips that are still Eli's, the original equipment, the unmodified feature   and the contact is soft and slow and deliberate. She tastes like soy sauce and mirin and the specific warmth of a person who cooked you dinner and heard your confession and stayed.

I kiss her back. My hand finds her waist   the curve beneath the sweater, the architecture I've been mapping in stolen moments. Her hand finds the back of my neck, fingers in the short hair at my nape, pulling gently, the gesture that means deeper. The kiss deepens.

The "mmnh." The sound she makes. The vibration against my lips, the involuntary music, the signal from the body that the brain didn't approve. I feel it and I'm alive in a way that has nothing to do with viewer counts or dashboards or the loop. I'm alive because a girl who knows the worst thing about me is kissing me like it doesn't matter.

The kiss lengthens. Her body shifts on the couch   closer, angling toward me, her knee coming up across my thigh, her weight redistributing from beside me to half-on-top-of-me. The sweater rides up at the back and my hand follows the exposed skin   warm, smooth, the curve of her lower back, the ridge of her spine beneath my fingertips.

She makes another sound. Not the "mmnh"   something deeper, something that starts in her chest and travels through the kiss into mine. Her fingers tighten at my nape. Her hips shift.

My body responds. Immediately. The same physics from last night   the same insistent, undeniable pressure, the body's argument that the brain has no jurisdiction over. She's close enough to feel it. She's positioned in a way that means she has to feel it   her thigh across my lap, the contact point where biology announces itself without consulting propriety.

She doesn't move away.

She breaks the kiss. Forehead against mine. Breathing harder. The glasses are somewhere   on the couch cushion, on the floor, departed from her face during the shift in geometry. Her eyes are large and close and brown and looking directly into mine.

<<kira "Hi.">>

<<eli "Hi.">>

<<kira "Is that   are you ">>

<<eli "Yeah. Sorry. I can't ">>

<<kira "Don't apologize.">>

Her hand moves. From my nape, down my chest, across my stomach   following the centerline, the specific geography of intention. Slow. Deliberate. Each inch of descent a question she's asking with her fingers instead of her voice.

Her hand reaches the waistband of my joggers. Stops. Her fingertips rest against the elastic, the boundary between above and below, the line that no one has crossed on me before   not Kira, not anyone, not ever.

<<kira "Is this okay?">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

<<kira "Are you sure?">>

<<eli "I'm very, very sure.">>

Her fingers slide beneath the elastic. The contact is warm and direct and the rest of the world falls away   the apartment, the couch, the cat, the flowers, the ceiling, the city, the platform, the algorithm, the $876, the nose, the truth, all of it, everything, gone. Reduced to one point of contact. One hand. One person who decided to touch me in a way that says I see all of you and I choose this.

Her fingers wrap around me. Warm. Small. The pixel-heart tattoo at her wrist disappearing beneath the fabric. She moves and the movement is slow at first, exploratory, learning the shape and the weight and the specific feedback of a body she hasn't touched before. Her grip adjusts   tighter, then softer, finding the pressure that makes my breath catch.

<<eli "Kira ">>

<<kira "Shh. Let me.">>

She lets her. Her hand moves with a rhythm that builds from tentative to confident, the same way she builds a stream   reading the audience, adjusting the approach, finding the cadence that produces the maximum response. Except the audience is me and the response is physical and she's reading it through the pulse she can feel in her palm and the sounds I'm making into the space between her neck and her shoulder.

I bury my face against her. She smells like shampoo and soy sauce and the floral body wash from last night's bathrobe appearance and underneath it all the specific scent of Kira's skin, warm and close, the scent I've been cataloging for eighteen months and am now experiencing at a resolution I never had access to.

The pace increases. Her wrist rotates slightly   a variation, an experiment, the response to a sound I make that she decides to chase. My hips move against her hand and the movement is involuntary, the body operating independently of the brain, the simple mechanics of a person receiving pleasure from another person for the first time in his life.

The build is fast. Embarrassingly fast. Months of burritos and solitude and a ceiling crack and then five days of kissing and bathrobe images and the "mmnh" and her hand, her actual hand, and the combination of novelty and longing and the specific tenderness of her touch creates a velocity that my body has no interest in managing.

<<eli "Kira, I'm   I'm going to ">>

<<kira "I know.">>

She doesn't slow down. She tightens. The movement becomes precise, focused, the final twenty seconds of attention that pushes everything over the edge.

I come. Into her hand, my face pressed against her neck, a sound leaving my mouth that I've never heard myself make   raw, involuntary, stripped of every performance and every persona. Not Eli's sound. Not Ellie's sound. Just the sound a body makes when another body gives it what it's been needing.

The wave rolls through and subsides. My breathing is ragged. My hands are gripping her sweater at the back, the fabric bunched in my fists. I'm trembling   the whole body, a fine vibration, the aftershock of something seismic happening in a small apartment on a Friday night in January.

She holds still. Her hand still, gentle now, not moving, just holding. She presses her lips to my temple.

<<kira "Hey. You okay?">>

<<eli "I'm   yeah. I'm... that was ">>

<<kira "Was it okay?">>

<<eli "Kira. That was the single greatest experience of my entire existence. Including the 1v3 clutch AND the ramen.">>

She laughs against my temple. The real one. The crinkle-nose one. The vibration of it goes through my skull and settles somewhere deep.

She pulls her hand free. Carefully. Reaches for the tissue box on the coffee table   the tissue box she has because she's a functional human being with a functional apartment   and cleans up. Gently. Practically. The combination of tenderness and pragmatism that is so specifically Kira that I want to archive the moment in every sensory format available.

<<kira "How do you feel?">>

<<eli "Like a person who just experienced something they're going to think about every single day for the rest of their life.">>

<<kira "Oh my god, stop.">>

<<eli "I'm serious. You can't just do that to a person and expect them to form normal sentences afterward.">>

She settles against my side. My arm goes around her. The position. The template. Her head on my shoulder, glasses back on (retrieved from the couch cushion), Packet reclaiming the opposite armrest with the air of a cat who waited politely until the humans were done being disgusting.

<<kira "Your nose really is pretty.">>

<<eli "You want to talk about my nose right now?">>

<<kira "I'm admiring my boyfriend's new nose after giving him a handjob on my couch. This is multitasking.">>

<<eli "You said boyfriend.">>

<<kira "I did.">>

<<eli "That's the first time you've said it.">>

<<kira "It's the first time you've deserved it. Telling the truth earns titles.">>

Her finger traces the new nose again. Bridge to tip. The line Dr. Maren drew, now Kira's route.

<<kira "The nose and the brows and the lashes. They're beautiful, Eli. Really. The work is incredible. But ">>

<<eli "But?">>

<<kira "Don't do anything you don't want. For streaming. For money. For me. If you're changing your face because it makes you feel something good   because looking in the mirror and seeing this nose makes you feel more like yourself, whoever that self is   that's one thing. But if you're doing it purely because chat asked for a face reveal and the donations scale with the modifications? That's a hamster wheel and hamster wheels don't have exits.">>

<<eli "I hear you.">>

<<kira "I'm bisexual. You becoming more feminine doesn't change what I feel. But not everyone in your life is bisexual. Marcus isn't. Your mom isn't. People will notice. People will talk and at some point the changes become harder to explain with 'grooming kit from the drugstore.'">>

<<eli "I know.">>

<<kira "So promise me. No modifications you don't genuinely want. No changes for the algorithm. Only changes for you.">>

<<eli "I promise.">>

She tilts her head up from my shoulder. Kisses me. Slow. Tasting like soy sauce and trust. The kind of kiss that isn't leading anywhere   the kind that's a conclusion instead of an introduction. A seal on a deal. A signature on a contract neither of us wrote but both of us just signed.

<<kira "Goodnight, disaster. Go home and stream.">>

<<eli "How do you know I'm streaming tonight?">>

<<kira "Because I know you and Ellie has a nose to show off.">>

She grins. The teasing one. The asymmetric one. My favorite.

<<eli "You're not weirded out by that? The Ellie thing?">>

<<kira "I am a little weirded out. But I'm also a streamer. I put on a persona every time I go live. The difference between KiraByte and Kira is real. The difference between ElliePlayz and Eli is just... bigger and newer and involves a wig.">>

<<eli "And a voice modulator.">>

<<kira "And a voice modulator. Which, by the way, I want to hear sometime.">>

<<eli "You want to hear Ellie's voice?">>

<<kira "I want to hear all of you. Including the parts you built.">>

The sentence lands in my chest and stays. All of you. Including the parts you built. Said by a person who means it. Said by a person who builds herself on stream every day and understands that the built thing can be as real as the raw thing, that the persona and the person aren't enemies but layers, that identity isn't a single note but a chord.

I stand. She walks me to the door. Packet watches from the couch, tail flicking once.

The doorway kiss. Long. Her hands on my chest, mine on her waist, the tiptoe, the rise, the warm press of her lips against mine. The taste of gyudon and the scent of cherry blossom from the air freshener in her car that has somehow permeated her entire life.

<<kira "Text me after the stream?">>

<<eli "Always.">>

<<kira "❤️">>

She says it out loud again. The word-as-emoji. The sound of a heart rendered in her voice, breathy and warm and aimed at me like a flashlight in a dark room.

<<eli "❤️">>

I say it back. Out loud. The first time I've spoken the symbol instead of typed it. The word feels different when your mouth makes it   physical, real, a sound that requires breath and intention and the specific vulnerability of saying "heart" to a person's face.

She smiles. The real one. Sunrise.

The door closes. The hallway. The elevator that sounds like a submarine. The cold.

I walk home. Fifteen minutes. January. But the cold is different tonight   it's the cold of a person walking TOWARD something instead of away from everything. The cold of a person with sweet-pea gratitude and a new nose and a cleared debt and a girlfriend who gave him a handjob after hearing the worst thing about him and called him "boyfriend" for the first time.

The cold is just cold. The rest is warm.

<<set $hint to "Go home and stream">>
[[Go Home|neighbourhood]]

